<!-- @before 2. OAuth 2.0 Authorization Code + PKCE (the Login) :: guide -->
### Bind the browser response before exchanging the code

Use an external user agent and the registered redirect contract. PKCE binds code exchange to the verifier; state and issuer/redirect checks address other mix-up and response-binding threats. Token types depend on the requested protocol, grants and provider: an ID token is an OpenID Connect result, not mandatory in every OAuth exchange. [Native-app OAuth](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8252.html) and [current security BCP](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9700.html) and [OpenID Connect token semantics](https://openid.net/specs/openid-connect-core-1_0.html#IDToken).

<!-- @before 5. Logout :: staff -->
### Local retirement and remote revocation have different completion rules

The source vault contract atomically rejects replacement after invalidation. Retire captured-session work and data without clearing a newer account. Best-effort revocation is not proof of account-wide logout; use the provider’s server session/revocation contract for that guarantee.
